{{- /*
  Four mise launcher stubs that must not exist in ~/.local/bin.

  Each runs `mise use -g`, which WRITES ~/.config/mise/config.toml -- a
  chezmoi-managed file. Every invocation therefore causes chezmoi drift: the
  tool works, and silently rewrites managed state behind chezmoi's back.

  Only these four are listed. They have a real mise install that PATH resolves
  ahead of the stub, so removing them costs nothing. The other nine stubs
  (copilot, crush, gemini, ghui, grok, hunk, omp, pi, playwright) are the ONLY
  launcher for their tool -- nothing in [tools] installs them -- so removing
  those would remove the tools. They keep the drift bug until they are either
  added to [tools] or dropped.

  WARNING -- not idempotent once a target is RECREATED. Verified on chezmoi
  v2.72.0: the first apply deletes silently, but if a stub comes back, the next
  apply prompts ("... has changed since chezmoi last wrote it") -- failing
  rc=1 with stdin closed, HANGING with stdin open. Omarchy migrations do write
  ~/.local/bin, so this is reachable.

  Non-interactive apply paths must therefore pass --force; tests/podman/harness.sh
  already does. [tasks.bootstrap] deliberately does NOT: it is run by a human at
  a terminal, and --force is global -- it suppresses the prompt for all 142
  managed targets, silently reverting any local edit. Answering one prompt is
  cheaper than losing the drift signal.
*/ -}}
.local/bin/claude
.local/bin/codex
.local/bin/gh
.local/bin/opencode
.local/share/icons/nushell.ico
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "windows" }}
AppData/Local/lazygit/config.yml
AppData/Local/lazygit/state.yml
{{- end }}
